<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigtuna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigtuna is a town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a> north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, founded around 980 as a royal centre. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a> struck the first Swedish coins there around 995, modelled on English pennies, and in the eleventh century it became an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat and a base for the Christianisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. It declined in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> and later Stockholm, but its medieval church ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigtuna.jpg" alt="Sigtuna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brorsson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stora_gatan_i_Sigtuna_nr_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
